      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective observational 5-year study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess pain, functional disability, surgical status, and health care use of patients who actively selected complementary and alternative medicine treatment and risk factors for lumbar surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Controversy continues regarding difference in long-term outcomes of conservative and surgical treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We recruited 150 consecutive lumbar disc herniation patients with radiating pain (numeric rating scale ≥5) from November, 2006 at a Korean medicine hospital outpatient department, of which 128 patients completed 6 months of complementary and alternative medicine treatment (herbal medicine, acupuncture, bee-venom pharmacopuncture, and Chuna manipulation). Follow-up data was collected every year for 5 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We assessed surgical status in 105 patients (82%), of which 8 replied that they had received surgery. Ninety-two patients (72%) attended the 5-year follow-up. Visual analog scale of back pain which was 4.19\u200a±\u200a2.60 at baseline improved after treatment, decreasing to 0.94\u200a±\u200a1.13 at 6 months, and was maintained at 1.25\u200a±\u200a1.81 at 5 years. Visual analog scale of leg pain decreased from 7.50\u200a±\u200a1.32 to 0.94\u200a±\u200a1.29 and was sustained at 0.98\u200a±\u200a1.73. Participants reported less disability with Oswestry Disability Index scores decreasing from 41.50\u200a±\u200a15.07 at baseline to 11.24\u200a±\u200a10.44 at 6 months, which then declined further to 7.61\u200a±\u200a9.82 at 5 years. SF-36 quality-of-life health survey scores also improved, increasing from 33.41\u200a±\u200a12.67 at baseline to 66.04\u200a±\u200a15.77 at 6 months, and reaching 75.43\u200a±\u200a15.79 at 5 years. In assessment of satisfaction with current state, 20% replied \"highly satisfied,\" 67% \"satisfied,\" 10% \"fairly satisfied,\" and 2% \"dissatisfied.\" Patients with younger age, previous treatment for current pain episode, and higher levels of sensory impairment and pain in the lower extremities were at higher risk of lumbar surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The long-term results of lumbar disc herniation patients receiving nonsurgical complementary and alternative medicine treatment were favorable and satisfaction rates were high."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "LEVEL OF EVIDENCE",
          "content": 2
        }
      ]},
